# Jupyter notebooks — workflow supplement

**Primary rule:** `.cursor/rules/notebook-blank-edit.mdc` (`alwaysApply: true`) — **prime directive:** line/cluster red/green for every notebook change; **burn-first** each user message unless `skip burn`; **ask** when unsure; **no** per-reply cell-count cap.

This file adds **540-specific** details only.

---

## Why tactics exist (not caps)

Cursor `EditNotebook` on `.ipynb` is **unreliable**: first call may auto-apply; chaining may hide later diffs; a **second** edit to the **same** cell in one reply often shows only “keep cell” without line detail. The **burn slot** sacrifices the first hit; **merging** all line changes in one call per cell per pass protects line-level review. The user may still edit **many cells** in one reply — goal is **all** changes reviewable, not fewer edits.

---

## `540_tenure_pipeline.ipynb`

- Burn slot: cell index **3** (not 1). Indices 0–2 are title / ToC / Cell 0 metadata — do not use for burn.
- After burn: **"Blank edit done — EDIT BLANK cell updated."**

```python
##### EDIT BLANK ###### DISCARD
# session: YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM — brief summary of this pass
# - Session marker: …  (older entries appended at bottom per notebook-blank-edit.mdc)
```

---

## Same cell — one `EditNotebook` per pass

If multiple lines in one cell change, use **one** `old_string` / `new_string` covering **all** of them. Do not make two content edits to the same cell in one reply if one merged edit can do the job.

---

## Tools

| Operation | Tool |
|-----------|------|
| Cell body edits | **`EditNotebook`** |
| Never for content | **`notebook_edit_cell`** (MCP) |
| Structural / MCP content | **Ask user first** (except bootstrap per `notebook-blank-edit.mdc`) |

---

## Common mistakes

1. Burn on **index 1** in 540 (wrong — use **3**).
2. Invisible burn (whitespace only) — use a real `# session:` update.
3. Announcing burn done when `EditNotebook` failed.
4. Shell + JSON to patch cell bodies (bypasses review UI).
5. Assuming user intent from scrolling “thought” text — **ask in the reply**.
